+The detector may instead be a rule the project's linter runs, written
+`via: lint <tool>:<rule>` or `via: lint <tool>:<rule> matching "<text>"`.
+The tool names an entry under `lint` in `coherence.config.json`, the rule is
+the tool's own id, and `matching` keeps only the findings whose message
+carries the text, because one rule id carries many patterns. It fits a scoped
+ban or a paved path over an external API, which no chokepoint can express:
+
+```markdown
+- ingestion never reaches cdp: Ingestion code depends on CDP only through the interfaces in ~/common.
+  over: every non-test import under nodejs/src/ingestion except the listed residual: legacy/replay.ts
+  via: lint oxlint:no-restricted-imports matching "~/cdp"
+  because: ingestion and CDP deploy apart, and a direct import couples their releases
+  kinds: none
+```
+
+The rule is total over the set the lint config names. What that config
+excludes (its ignore patterns, its per-file ignores) is the listed residual,
+and `over:` names the same files; there is no count and no stored list of
+known findings. It is refuted like any totality oracle: stage a finding, run
+`refute`, restore, run. A value that begins with `lint` and does not read in
+this form is a spec problem, never taken as a test name.
+
 `because` says why the invariant exists and what it protects against.
 `crossing` is the security marker: two trust levels the entry spec declares.
 `refuted` is the human account of a witnessed firing: what was broken, what
